  "schema_version": "bookstory.v1",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The Elemental Analysis of Glass Beads has achieved clear scholarly traction since its 2022 publication, with 12 citations placing it in the top 10% of cited works for its field and year. Its open-access availability and 3,567 recorded downloads suggest sustained use by researchers working on glass composition, bead studies, and archaeological materials analysis.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Laure Dussubieux and Heather Walder’s book has already established itself as a useful reference point for researchers studying glass beads through compositional analysis. The citation record shows measurable academic uptake, and the citing literature is concentrated in archaeology and cultural heritage science, especially in journals and venues that focus on materials characterization, provenance, and bead studies. That pattern indicates the book is being used not only as background reading but as a methodological resource for subsequent analytical work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The reach of the book extends beyond citation counts alone. With 3,567 downloads reported through OAPEN, the volume has seen substantial direct use, consistent with an open-access book that is being consulted by scholars and students. The publication is also associated with KU Leuven support, and its SDG alignment with Life below water suggests relevance to broader questions of exchange, movement, and material culture connected to aquatic routes and maritime histories.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape shows a steady flow of follow-on research across 2022–2026, with especially frequent uptake in Archaeometry. Recent citing works include studies on glass beads from Mission San José, medieval and postmedieval Ethiopia, Southeast Asia, the East African coast, and southwestern Taiwan, alongside work on glass bangles from Yemen and a comparative chapter on blue European glass. This spread suggests the book’s analytical approach has become part of an active, international research conversation on glass composition and provenance.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record is strongest for scholarly influence and repository-based access. There is also a modest public-discourse signal in the form of Crossref-linked mentions, but event data are otherwise absent in the available record, so the evidence here is best understood as research-facing rather than broadly public-facing. Overall, the book shows a well-defined early impact profile: strong specialist uptake, meaningful open-access use, and clear integration into subsequent archaeometric and archaeological studies of glass beads.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}
